 are no signals really out there  saying that you need to stop borrowing  right now  america's grim experience of covert 19  triggered a deep recession  but it will still probably be the best  performing g7 economy in 2020  the future does not belong to globalists  the future belongs to patriots the  future belongs to sovereign and  independent nations  [Music]  president trump promised to put america  first an anti-globalist stance which he  wasted  no time in implementing pulling america  out of 10  international organizations or treaties  he promised to renegotiate all of  america's trade deals his tactics seemed  to be threatening  other countries with tariffs and and you  know essentially trying to  trying to squeeze concessions out of  them the fact is  nafta has been a disaster  for the united states a complete and  total  disaster his critics said that this is a  very very dangerous  strategy because the risk is you toxify  politics  uh in the other countries that you're  dealing with and so if you enter a  negotiation essentially promising to  bully the other side into  submission the risk is it just kind of  blows up in your face  yet clearly there were all these deals  he renegotiated a deal with mexico and  canada  and a deal with south korea and then  there's  china starting in 2018  america waged a trade war with china the  average tariff rate on goods travelling  between america and china has since more  than tripled  if trump came in promising to in some  ways reset  the relationship with china when it  comes to trade he has managed to do that  we can disagree about whether all of it  is in a good way but he's  he's reset it the two countries agreed  the first phase of a trade deal in  january 2020  which included an agreement from china  to lift some trade barriers  and to buy certain american products but  right now  it doesn't look like china will meet  those commitments  the other thing there are still tariffs  on hundreds of billions of dollars worth  of  chinese exports to america and looking  ahead  i think those will be quite difficult to  roll back  entirely the u.s china relationship  has been changed and in a way that is  not going to be very easy to  reverse um certainly not politically  we will build a great wall along the  southern border  and mexico will pay for the wall  [Music]  it's one of the pledges that launched  trump's campaign and helped win him the  white house  so how much impact has trump had on  immigration  and so mexico has not paid for the wall  and he has not built much new wall but  the wall in the southern border is real  and he has taken funds from the defense  department to pay for it he has also  changed the asylum system he has made it  much harder  for people fleeing persecution to claim  asylum at the southern border  i think last year for the first time  canada overtook the united states  as the country that settles the most  refugees  trump's administration slashed the  number of refugee admissions from 108  000 by the end of 2016 to 24  000 by the end of 2019  under trump the number of migrants in  detention centers has increased  the tone and intention of his policies  has been clear  and unwavering children and adults are  seen huddled together in the building  that's divided up into chain-link  fencing that looks like a cage  it was a statement that immigration was  something that was not desirable  and that has been the main focus of his  presidency many of the policies that  grabbed the headlines were enacted by  executive orders signed by the president  without needing to be passed through  congress  this is border security immigration  enforcement  and improvements very important we've  been talking about this right from the  beginning  which means in theory they could be  reversed by the same process  that may be true as a technical matter  but the extent to which he has  changed america's stance on immigration  the extent which  he has turned the republican party away  from the long-standing  bipartisan consensus that we are a  nation of immigrants and that's a source  of strength and pride  can't be so easily undone also the sheer  number of changes he's made  is going to be really hard for a biden  administration if indeed that's what we  have  to contend with because it's going to be  coming into office  and having to deal with a depressed  economy with a pandemic  with probably calls for government  reform there's just a limited amount of  political capital  so obama's talking about all of this  with the global warming and  that a lot of it's a hoax it's a hoax a  lot of it  trump announced america would withdraw  from the paris climate agreement  though other countries have sought to  move away from fossil fuels  trump set about reigniting america's oil  natural gas and coal industries  environmental protection agency and the  department of the interior have really  been firing on all cylinders in terms of  removing  regulations on on coal plants not only  but also you know methane  pipelines and um exploration of  wilderness the cumulative effect that  there's  you know some people have measured that  this would increase carbon dioxide  emissions by  three percent in a year over time uh  which is  substantial it's at a time when you know  you want america to be  going the opposite direction especially  because you want other countries to feel  obliged to also fall in that direction  in total trump has reversed 79  environmental rules  including the clean power plan which had  set limits on carbon emissions from  electricity plants  replacing it with a more lenient rule  many of the rollbacks are facing legal  challenges by states and environmental  groups  but these might fail the thing that's  that's tricky about this  if trump were to be elected and  litigation continues  and there's a chance that some of  trump's changes would become permanent  and perhaps more damaging than that the  supreme court could rule to limit the  epa's authority to regulate greenhouse  gas emissions  so the environmental protection agency  which serves  under the president would be limited  under all future presidents even a  democratic one from having really bold  environmental regulations  [Music]  you know what i got in we had  over a hundred federal judges that  weren't appointed  it was like a big beautiful president to  all of us  trump has appointed 217 federal judges  including 161 to district courts  53 to courts of appeal and three to the  supreme court  it is my honor to nominate amy  coney barrett in all  trump has appointed more than a quarter  of the judiciary  he's moved faster than any president in  their first term  for 40 years the appointment or  elevation of so many conservative judges  is already reshaping american law  many will sit for life so the impact not  least in limiting the scale and scope of  government legislation  could be felt for decades to come  probably  most pronouns are going to be at the  supreme court there's a sort of  long-standing worry that  roe vs wade which is the supreme court  case that gave women a constitutional  right to an abortion  will be limited um even further some  people think it might even be  repealed entirely there's another  challenge to the affordable care act  up before them on november 10th there's  also a lot of fear about  you know things happening along any of  these dimensions that could um  hamper a sort of subsequent democratic  administration which is likely to  try and use the machinery of the  administrative state to sort of  achieve its policy ambitions and the  idea is that a very sort of trump-heavy  supreme court  and trump heavy courts generally are  just going to limit that  quite a lot  [Music]  perhaps trump's most enduring legacy  will go far beyond  policy when you report fake news you are  the enemy of the people  four years of lies and bullying have  inflamed political discourse in america  partisanship and polarization have been  increasing for many  decades before trump came out of the  scene you can say that trump is sort of  a symptom  of that polarization but that he's also  accelerated it in some ways  obama was seen as hugely divisive trump  is seen as divisive the difference is  barack obama made conscious efforts to  reach out to people who disagree  with him donald trump has not done that  and so  even to the extent that every president  is divisive he has reveled in the  divisiveness  in a way that past presidents have not  like to punch him in the face i'll tell  you  so if you look at sort of measures of  how republicans feel about democrats and  democrats feel about republicans you  look at whether people talk about the  other party as evil or immoral  all these sorts of kind of extreme  measures they all go up during the  trump administration a recent survey  found that one in five americans  believed violence could be justified to  some extent if the other party wins the  impending election  but america isn't just fractured along  political lines  and you see that the republican parties  increasingly become  a party of white identity if you  identify as having  racially conservative views on  african-americans you're much more  likely to become a republican  now than ever before trump has just  accelerated the polarization along these  identity lines  and even if he loses it will still be  the case  if he wins obviously we have another  four years to assess his legacy  if he loses i i think that he exposed  and  invigorated a part of american culture  that  um some had ignored in the past and i  don't think that that's going away  i'm samia keynes the economist's trade  and globalization editor  you can follow our coverage of the 2020  election and what happens next  by clicking on the link opposite thanks  for watching  you

